{
  "file": "src/authoring/progress.rs",
  "item": "mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_right]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This unit-test-only TermLike adapter records whole rendered lines.\n Indicatif's authoring renderer does not request horizontal movement,\n so this required no-op callback has no application input path.\n The authoring PTY tests exercise the real terminal implementation."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/authoring/progress.rs#mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_right]` is on an explicit function with a body, which the built-in allows. Its fenced commentary is a substantive reason, not just a note that the code is hard to cover. The function is a required no-op method of a unit-test-only `TermLike` adapter (`RecordingTerm`) that records whole lines. The source agrees with the claims. The production code, `BatchProgress::new`, `inspect` and `refresh`, only creates a `ProgressBar` on `console::Term::buffered_stderr()`, sets styles and messages, and draws. It never moves the cursor horizontally itself. The test adapter is not used on any application path. The commentary also says the authoring PTY tests exercise the real terminal. They exist: `tests/authoring_batch.rs#impl[Fixture]/fn[run_in_pty_with_args]` and `tests/execution_coverage.rs#fn[coverage_progress_on_a_pty_preserves_reports_and_plain_output_policy]` both run progress output on a real PTY. I did not open the indicatif source, so the claim that its renderer never requests horizontal movement rests on my recollection of its API, not on a check. I found no contradiction in the code I read.",
